```{r}
#| label: fig-eberhart-russell
#| fig-cap: "Eberhart and Russell (1966) regression analysis"


ERm <- metan::ge_reg(
  .data   = droplevels(df_filter), 
  env     = "env",
  gen     = "gen",
  rep     = "rep",
  resp    = "GY",
  verbose = FALSE
)

top_gen <- ERm$GY$regression |>
  arrange(desc(b0)) |>
  slice(1:30) |> 
  pull(GEN) |>
  as.character()

top_gen <- unique(c(top_gen, "G318"))

ggplot(data = subset(ERm$GY$data, GEN %in% top_gen),
       aes(x = IndAmb, y = Y)) +
  facet_wrap(. ~ GEN) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x, se = FALSE, colour = "#10342d") +
  geom_point(aes(colour = ENV), size = 2) +
  theme_bw(base_size = 14) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dotted") +
  labs(x = "Environmental Index", 
       y = "Grain Yield (GY)", 
       colour = "Environment") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo") +
  stat_regline_equation()
```
As illustrated in @fig-eberhart-russell, the Eberhart and Russell regression model was applied to assess the adaptability and phenotypic stability of the top 30 highest-yielding genotypes. While this methodology provides valuable insights into how genotypes respond to environmental variations, a critical examination of the data reveals a significant methodological limitation regarding the number of testing environments.

A importat statistical artifact is present among several genotypes that were selected in this top 30 productivity ranking, specifically G114, G115, G191, G300, G317, G322, and G324. These materials were evaluated in only two environments. Mathematically, a linear regression line will always perfectly connect two points. Consequently, there is no dispersion around the line, meaning the deviation from regression ($S^2_{di}$) equals zero by definition. Although this perfect fit might falsely suggest flawless phenotypic stability, it is entirely a mathematical artifact resulting from a lack of degrees of freedom, rather than true biological resilience. Furthermore, this artifact heavily distorts the estimation of their regression parameters, generating unrealistic mean yields ($\beta_0$) and extreme slopes, such as the artificially inflated responsiveness of G191 ($\beta_1 = 10$).

In contrast, evaluating genotypes across a robust multi-environment network captures the genuine biological complexity of G×E interactions. Genotype G244, which is one of the most extensively tested materials in the network, clearly demonstrates this reality. Despite maintaining a competitive mean yield ($\beta_0 = 2700$ kg/ha), its data points are widely scattered around the regression line. This substantial dispersion indicates high unpredictability and a significant interaction with specific environmental factors that are not linearly explained by the environmental index.

Genotypes such as G335, G365, and G310 present a large number of data points that closely follow their respective regression lines, proving that high predictability is biologically attainable when adequately tested.
